  "Name": "Raut2011-Fig6a-SiO2-Si-SiO2-on-Si",
  "Source": "H. K. Raut, V. A. Ganesh, A. S. Nair, and S. Ramakrishna, \"Anti-reflective coatings: A critical, in-depth review,\" Energy Environ. Sci. 4, 3779-3804 (2011). doi:10.1039/c1ee01297e - Fig. 6(a) and caption, p. 3783 (layer labels SiO2 123 nm / Si 47.8 nm / SiO2 123 nm / Si active medium; caption: Si(RI 3.76)|SiO2(RI 1.455)|Si|SiO2|Air, layers lambda/4 thick at lambda0 = 720 nm, nH = 3.76, nL = 1.455); text p. 3784, Sec. 6.2.3 (ref. 20, Macleod)",
  "Notes": "Simulated quarter-wave L-H-L multilayer on a Si absorber (transmissivity into Si), the reference case for Fig. 6(b)\nSpecification: Analysis of transmissivity in the far-red zone 720-750 nm (p. 3784).\nReported: Only a transmissivity curve (Fig. 6(a)) with unreadable axis numbers. Expected from the printed quarter-wave construction: an L-H-L quarter stack on n = 3.76 gives admittance 0.084 at 720 nm, i.e. T into Si only about 29 % (a quick recomputation with the printed nm values gives T = 0.287 at 720 nm, versus 0.664 for bare Si), so this stack is a poor AR - it is the comparison case for Fig. 6(b).\nNotes: Paper lists the stack from the substrate (Si | SiO2 | Si | SiO2 | Air); here reversed so layer 1 is next to air (the stack is symmetric, so the order is the same). The Si film and the Si substrate both use n = 3.76 (nH = n_active medium = 3.76); all indices constant, no absorption, as in the simulation. Printed thicknesses 123 nm and 47.8 nm are rounded quarter waves at 720 nm (exact: 123.71 nm and 47.87 nm). Transmissivity means transmittance into the semi-infinite Si (= 1 - R).\nLayers from Fig. 6(a) and caption, p. 3783 (layer labels SiO2 123 nm / Si 47.8 nm / SiO2 123 nm / Si active medium; caption: Si(RI 3.76)|SiO2(RI 1.455)|Si|SiO2|Air, layers lambda/4 thick at lambda0 = 720 nm, nH = 3.76, nL = 1.455); text p. 3784, Sec. 6.2.3 (ref. 20, Macleod); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours T 51.0 % at 480 nm, 28.7 % at 720 nm; paper comparison case for Fig. 6b (curve only).",
